<subsection xml:id="sec-14-16-24-all-calculators">
  <title>Calculators</title>

  <p> Full participation in this course requires the use of a scientific calculator.  It is <alert>recommended</alert> that you purchase one, especially if you will be taking additional math, physics, chemistry, engineering, or business courses, so that you can practice using it and become familiar with it prior to taking your exams.</p>  
  <p> However, purchase is not strictly necessary.  Free online calculators are available and <alert>SOME</alert> physical calculators can be borrowed from your instructor, if testing in class, or the exam center (check with your instructor or proctor first).</p>

    <paragraphs><title>Free Online Calculators</title></paragraphs>

<ul>
    <li><url href="https://www.desmos.com/scientific">Desmos Scientific Calculator</url></li>
    <li><url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator">Desmos Graphing Calculator</url> (not recommended for regular use but is good to use to check whether you graphed a function correctly)</li>
    <li><url href="https://www.geogebra.org/scientific?lang=en">Geogebra Scientific Calculator</url></li>
    <li><url href="https://scientificcalculator.site/">Casio-Style Scientific Calculator</url></li>
    <li><url href="https://tcsion.com/OnlineAssessment/ScientificCalculator/Calculator.html">General Scientific Calculator</url></li>
</ul>

  <p>It is common that students use calculators in class or on assignments that are not allowed on assessments. You will not be prevented from doing so but you will be asked to put them away when working on proctored assessments. Below are non-exhaustive lists of allowed and disallowed calculators.</p>
  


<paragraphs><title>Allowed: Scientific Calculators</title></paragraphs>
<p>A scientific calculator is an calculator used for math, science, and engineering. It can perform advanced functions like trigonometry, logarithms, exponents, and square roots, in addition to basic calculations.</p>


